    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
      <section class="mb-12">
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Production designers, art directors, and event prop builders in the Klang Valley share one bottleneck: lightweight, paintable, easy-to-shape sheet material that can survive a shoot or one-night event without breaking the budget. Plywood is heavy and slow to cut. Foam board is lightweight but flimsy. Corrugated cardboard sheets — especially BC double-wall — sit in the sweet spot. They are the workhorse of Malaysian film sets, music video shoots, theatre productions, brand activations, and TVC studio builds. Here is how the productions we supply use them.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="font-headline text-2xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-6">Why Corrugated Cardboard Beats the Alternatives on Set</h2>
        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Speed of Build</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">A flat wall in plywood needs measuring, sawing, sanding, and screwing — at least an hour with two people. The same wall in scored BC cardboard takes 15 minutes with a utility knife and a metre stick. For productions with 24-hour build windows before a shoot, that speed determines whether the set is ready or not.</p>

        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Weight & Crew Safety</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">A 1.2m × 2.4m plywood sheet weighs around 18kg. The same dimensions in BC double-wall cardboard weighs 2-3kg. That changes everything for solo production assistants moving sets between studios in KL, climbing scaffolds for fly walls, and dressing tight stage spaces. Less injury risk, less hire cost on labour, faster turnover.</p>

        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Disposal After Wrap</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Plywood from a wrapped set goes to landfill or eats studio storage indefinitely. Cardboard sets fold flat and ship to a recycler the same day. For sustainability-conscious productions and brands running ESG-linked campaigns, that closes the loop on set construction emissions.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="font-headline text-2xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-6">Common Applications on Malaysian Productions</h2>
        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Set Walls & Backdrop Flats</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">BC double-wall sheets framed onto 2×2 timber battens give plywood-equivalent rigidity. Score and fold a 90-degree corner for self-standing walls. Paint with primer-plus-acrylic for matched matte finishes that cinematographers love because they do not bounce hot light back at the lens.</p>

        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Custom Props — Furniture, Vehicles, Architecture</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Cardboard cars, period furniture, fake walls of historical buildings — production designers cut them from 7mm BC sheets and skin with the appropriate finish. The Malaysian Filem TV scene and KLPAC theatre productions have used Quinocycle sheets for everything from oversized 1950s telephones to a full-scale cardboard kampung house facade.</p>

        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Brand Activation & Trade Show Builds</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">For one-day brand events at MITEC, KLCC, or shopping mall atriums, cardboard sets are unbeatable on cost and setup speed. We supply pre-printed sheets for activations needing logo coverage, with sustainability messaging baked into the visual identity.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="font-headline text-2xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-6">Practical Build Tips from Set Builders We Supply</h2>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Always score before folding — never just bend. Scoring with a blunt screwdriver tip or a back of a knife creates the crease line and prevents the kraft layer tearing. For paint, prime before colour or the cardboard absorbs unevenly and creates patchy finish. Reinforce stress points (door cutouts, hanging fixture mounts) with a strip of timber lath glued behind the cardboard surface — distributes the load across more area.</p>
      </section>

      <div class="bg-surface-variant/50 border border-outline-variant/20 rounded-lg p-8 my-12">
